POST /api/auth/code/send
- Body: { purpose: "signup" | "login", resend: boolean }.
- First decide whether a code is needed (Rule 2). If not, return { required: false, reason: "trusted_device" }, send nothing, and for owner emails restore the owner claim server-side.
- If needed: if an unexpired, unused code exists and resend is false, do NOT send again; return the current state (idempotent, so refreshes and double effects never create a second email). If resend is true, enforce the cooldown (429 { error: "cooldown", resendAvailableAt, serverTime }). Otherwise create a new code, which invalidates any old one.
- Code: crypto.randomInt(0, 1000000), padded to 6 digits. Store only HMAC-SHA256(code, per-code random salt + CODE_HMAC_SECRET) with the salt in Firestore collection authCodes/{uid} (server-only, rules deny all client access): hash, salt, purpose, createdAt, expiresAt (10 minutes), resendAvailableAt (60 seconds), attemptsLeft (5), used (false).
- Limits (inside Firestore transactions so several instances agree): max 5 codes per hour per uid; max 20 sends per hour per IP; express-rate-limit burst limit; 60 second cooldown. Hitting a limit returns 429 { error: "hourly_limit" | "cooldown" | "rate_limited", retryAfter, resendAvailableAt }.
- Sending uses the shared mail function (Part 3). Return success ONLY after the provider accepted the message. If sending fails, invalidate that code and return 502 { error: "send_failed" } (users see "We couldn't send the code. Try again."). Never return the code.
- Success: { required: true, sent: true, alreadyActive: boolean, serverTime, expiresAt, resendAvailableAt, attemptsLeft } (epoch milliseconds).

POST /api/auth/code/verify
- Body: { code: "6 digits", trustDevice: boolean }. Validate with /^\d{6}$/.
- In a Firestore transaction: reject if there is no code, it is used, or it is expired (401/410 with the reason); if attemptsLeft is 0 return 429 { error: "locked" }; compare the HMAC with crypto.timingSafeEqual; on a wrong code decrement attemptsLeft and return { error: "wrong_code", attemptsLeft, serverTime }; at 0 attempts mark it dead. IP rate limit on verify too.
